  "schema:dateModified": "2019-02-20",
  "schema:description": "This metadata refers to the Land Cover vector data generated over Manovo-Gounda-St Floris-Bamingui (Africa) for 2013 in the framework of the Copernicus Global Land Hot Spot Mapping (C-GL-HSM) contract under the coordination of JRC. This area of interest is either mapped with the generic 8 classes dichotomus legend (CAF_06_lc_a) or the detailed modular legend (CAF_06_lc_b). The mapped area of interest (AOI) represents a Key Landscape for Conservation area (KLC). The KLC has a total size of about 8.5 million ha (85,000 km²) and is situated in the Central African Republic, partly extending into Chad. It contains the protected areas of Aouk-Aoukale faunal reserve, Gribingui-Bamingui faunal reserve, Koukourou-Bamingui faunal reserve, Bamingui-Bangoran National Park, Manovo-Gounda St Floris National Park, Avakaba Presidential Park, Ouandja Vakaga faunal reserve. Reference time: 2015 - 2019\n\nSupplemental information: The Manovo-Gounda St Floris National Park in the eastern part of the KLC is listed as UNESCO World heritage site. The uniqueness of the park derives from its wealth of flora and fauna, encompassing two ecological zones, the Sudano-Sahelian and Sudano-Guinean zone. This results in a variety of habitats from grassy plains in the north to savannas with gallery forests in the south. The park is surrounded by hunting areas and with a functional corridor to the National Park of Bamingui-Bangoran in the western part of the KLC protects the largest savanna of central Africa. The region is sparsely populated; however the park is under sever threat from poaching (notably of rhinoceros, elephant and giraffe) and grazing by nomad pastoralists from the Sudanese region of Nyala and from Chad, with 30 – 40,000 head of cattle, entering the park every winter.",
